e the whole class.\n\n  Line 49: Case: a store file reachable through a link is accepted by containment and then re-opened by its link name, so the reported `storePath` can differ from the file actually read.\n\n\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/lineages/render-hooks-plugin/review-report.md:\n  Line 75: - Evidence: `storePath = containment.path` records the checked path; `readFileSync(transcriptPath, 'utf8')` opens the caller's path. `pi.mjs:246-256` closes the same gap by reading the guard's resolved path.\n\n  Line 76: - Impact: `source.storePath` can name a file other than the one whose bytes produced the records.\n\n  Line 78: - Disposition: open · Scope proof: `rg -n \"storePath = containment.path\"` returns exactly the three readers\n\n\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/lineages/accuracy-runner-2/iterations/iteration-005.md:\n  Line 37: 1. **A file name git quotes cannot be restored, so one space in a created path stops the arm and leaves the file** -- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/worktree-restore.mjs:54` -- `statusSnapshot` reads `git status --porcelain` and keys each path with `line.slice(3)`. Git quotes a path that contains a space (probe inside the lineage: `?? \"a b.txt\"`), so the map key is `\"a b.txt\"` including both quote characters. `restorePath` then asks `git cat-file -e 'HEAD:\"a b.txt\"'`, which is not a path git knows; `git rm --cached --ignore-unmatch -- '\"a b.txt\"'` also matches nothing; and `rmSync(resolve(cwd, '\"a b.txt\"'))` deletes a differently named file at most. The turn's file therefore stays, `leftoverPaths` reports the quoted key, and `restoreTurn` returns `restored: false`, so `runTurn` ends the arm with `restore_failed`. The failure is safe but u