{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794670205703,"sessionID":"ses_e255ff46fffe8tw0MjspShTXDB","part":{"partID":"prt_1daae1917002uyXZJHEE0qnaQQ","sessionID":"ses_e255ff46fffe8tw0MjspShTXDB","messageID":"msg_1daadde500014jIMVPatMfFSz7","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-b4b6af6a3c21fafd","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"grep -n \"T014\\|T006\\|T003\" -A 4 specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/021-brief-head-parity/tasks.md | head -60"},"output":"48:Worker lanes. Lane H owns `HR` and `HRT` and runs T003. Lane R owns `RN` and `DBR` and runs T004 then T005. Lane P owns `PJ` and `PJT` and runs T006 then T014. Lane D owns `RM` and `HK` and starts after T004. The lanes own different files and run in parallel after T002. `[P]` marks a task that may start beside another lane's task. T005 and T014 carry no `[P]`, because each waits for the task before it in its lane.\n49-<!-- /ANCHOR:notation -->\n50-\n51----\n52-\n--\n68:- [x] T003 [P] Keep the command on the head row under every request shape (`HR`, `HRT`). Test first: in `HRT`, in the case `lets the top hub carry a command only a lower row would offer`, replace the last assertion `expect(everyRow.find((row) => row.skillId === 'sk-doc')?.command).toBeUndefined();` with `expect(everyRow[0]?.skillId).toBe('sk-doc');`, `expect(everyRow[0]?.command).toBe(command);` and `expect(everyRow[0]?.command).toBe(topOnly[0]?.command);`, and rename the case to `lets the top hub carry its command under every request shape`. Leave the case `never offers one command through both its own row and the owning hub` as it is, because it pins a hub that is not the head. Run `VX tests/handlers/advisor-recommend-compiled-route-option.vitest.ts` and see the renamed case fail with `everyRow[0].command` undefined. Then in `HR`'s `enrichCompiledRoutes`, replace `(topOnly && index === 0)` with `index === 0`, and replace the three comment lines that start `// A caller that asks for the top pick alone renders that head` with a comment saying every caller renders the head and at most one partner, so a command only a lower row carries would never reach the reader, and the head keeps it whichever shape the caller asked for. Verify: the first run fails as described, then `VX tests/handlers/advisor-recommend-compiled-route-option.vitest.ts` reports 0 failed, `rg -c \"topOnly && index === 0\" $HR` prints nothing and `TC` exits 0. When the untouched case fails because its hub now leads, mark the task `[B]` with the row order printed. Evidence: worker log: first run `Tests  1 failed | 6 passed (7)`, after the change `Tests  7 passed (7)`. Opus check PASS 2026-11-14: `VX` rerun `Tests  7 passed (7)`, `rg -c \"topOnly && index === 0\"` printed nothing, `TC` exit 0, the hub that is not the head is still suppressed.\n69-\n70-### Lane R: the renderer\n71-- [x] T004 [P] Print the policy word on the companion line (`RN`, `DBR`). Test first: in `DBR`, import `commandRowFor` from `../lib/command-attach.js` when absent. In the case `keeps the complete companion before directives under the default cap`, change the expected head to `` `Advisor: live; use sk-git 0.82/0.20 pass.\\nAlso: /doctor:git setup (${commandRowFor(<the bridge id that case passes>)?.policy})` ``, and in the case `uses the first doctor bridge after the top pick, past other skills`, change `'\\nAlso: /doctor:update'` to `'\\nAlso: /doctor:update (ask)'`. Add `it('prints the policy word of a companion and the child marker in a child session')`, which renders `advisorResult([{ skill: 'system-spec-kit', kind: 'skill', confidence: 0.95, uncertainty: 0.12 }, { skill: 'command-doctor-env', kind: 'command', confidence: 0.82, uncertainty: 0.2 }])` twice, with `{ childSession: false }` and with `{ childSession: true }`, expecting `\\nAlso: /doctor:env list (run)` both times because a read-only run stays visible to a child, and renders the same head with `command-doctor-update` under `{ childSession: true }` expecting `\\nAlso: /doctor:update (parent-named only)`. Run `VX tests/doctor-brief-render.vitest.ts` and see the three expectations fail on the missing policy word. Then in `RN`'s `companionCommandLabel`, build the label as the command followed by ` (<word>)`, where the word is `policyWord(row?.policy, row?.mutating, childSession)`, and return the command alone when that word is `null`. Replace the comment above the function with one saying a companion is a command the reader may act on, so it carries the same policy word the head carries, and a child still gets the downgrade marker. Verify: the first run fails as described, then `VX tests/doctor-brief-render.vitest.ts`, `VX tests/render-child-session.vitest.ts`, `VX tests/command-head-length.vitest.ts` and `VX tests/command-brief-golden.vitest.ts` report 0 failed and `TC` exits 0. When a golden brief in another file pins a companion with no policy word, update that one expectation and name the file in the task note. Evidence: worker log: first run `Tests  3 failed | 6 passed (9)` on the missing policy word, then `Tests  9 passed (9)`. Opus check PASS 2026-11-14: `VX` reruns `doctor-brief-render` 9 of 9, `render-child-session` 12 of 12, `command-head-length` 120 of 120, `command-brief-golden` 13 of 13, `TC` exit 0, no other golden file holds `Also: /`.\n72-- [x] T005 Drop a companion that repeats a printed command (`RN`, `DBR`). After T004. Test first: in `DBR`, add `it('prints a command once when the head already names it')`, which renders a head row `{ skill: 'system-skilled-doctor', kind: 'skill', confidence: 0.82, uncertainty: 0.26, command: '/doctor:update', policy: 'ask', ownerSkillId: 'system-skilled-doctor' }` followed by `{ skill: 'command-doctor-update', kind: 'command', confidence: 0.82, uncertainty: 0.2 }`, widening `RecommendationInput` with the three optional fields, and expects the brief to contain `/doctor:update` exactly once and no `Also:`. Add `it('prints a second doctor bridge when the first repeats the head')`, with a third row `command-doctor-env`, expecting `\\nAlso: /doctor:env list (run)`. Run `VX tests/doctor-brief-render.vitest.ts` and see the first case print `Also: /doctor:update (ask)`. Then in `RN`'s `renderAdvisorBrief`, compute the head's printed command as `top.command ?? (top.needsMode === true ? undefined : commandRowFor(top.skill)?.command)`, and make the companion pick skip a row whose `doctorCommandString(row.skill)` equals it. In the ambiguous branch, also print no companion whose command equals the partner's `vs.command ?? commandRowFor(vs.skill)?.command`. Add a comment saying a command printed twice reads as two offers. Verify: the first run fails as described, then `VX tests/doctor-brief-render.vitest.ts` and `VX tests/command-brief-golden.vitest.ts` report 0 failed and `TC` exits 0. Evidence: worker log: first run `Tests  2 failed | 9 passed (11)`, one on `[ '/doctor:update', '/doctor:update' ]` printed twice, then 24 of 24 across the two files. Opus check PASS 2026-11-14: `VX tests/doctor-brief-render.vitest.ts` 11 of 11, `tests/command-brief-golden.vitest.ts` 13 of 13, `TC` exit 0, a render probe printed no repeated companion and kept a distinct one.\n--\n75:- [x] T006 [P] Label the OpenCode head from the first passing row (`PJ`, `PJT`). Test first: in `PJT`, add `test('names the first passing row when the first row does not pass')`, which saves and deletes `process.env.AI_SESSION_CHILD`, builds the plugin with `makePlugin({ spawnOverride: spawnSequence([fakeChild({ stdout: cliEnvelope({ recommendations: [{ skillId: 'command-doctor-sk-skill-convert', score: 0.5, confidence: 0.8, uncertainty: 0.42, command: '/doctor:sk-skill convert', policy: 'ask', ownerSkillId: 'sk-doc' }, { skillId: 'sk-doc', score: 0.8, confidence: 0.95, uncertainty: 0.12 }] }) })]) })`, awaits `runPrompt(hooks)`, restores the variable in a `finally` and asserts that `output.system[0]` contains `use sk-doc 0.95/0.12 pass.` and does not contain `command-doctor-`. Run `VP` and see it fail with `use command-doctor-sk-skill-convert 0.95/0.12 pass.` Then in `PJ`, in the function that parses the CLI payload, replace `const top = recommendations[0] ?? null;` with `const top = recommendations.find((recommendation) => recommendation.passes_threshold) ?? recommendations[0] ?? null;` and add a comment saying the renderer leads with the first passing row, so a label taken from a row that does not pass would name one row and score another. Verify: the first run fails as described, then `VP` reports `fail 0`, `node --check $PJ` exits 0 and `rg -c \"const top = recommendations\\[0\\] \\?\\? null;\" $PJ` prints nothing. Evidence: worker log: first run `ℹ fail 1` with `Advisor: live; use command-doctor-sk-skill-convert 0.95/0.12 pass.`, then `ℹ pass 44`, `ℹ fail 0`. Opus check PASS 2026-11-14: `VP` rerun 44 of 44, `node --check` exit 0, the old `const top` line gone. Accepted deviation: the case passes `freshness: 'live'`, as every other brief case in `PJT` does, because without it the plugin renders the outage line and the case fails for the wrong reason.\n76:- [x] T014 Send the top-only route option from the OpenCode plugin (`PJ`, `PJT`). After T006. This task changes the function `advisorCliRequest` and nothing else. Test first: in `PJT`, add `test('asks the advisor for the route of the top pick alone, as the hooks do')`, which builds `const child = fakeChild({ stdout: cliEnvelope({ freshness: 'live', recommendations: [] }) });` and `const hooks = await makePlugin({ spawnOverride: spawnSequence([child]) });`, awaits `runPrompt(hooks)` and asserts `assert.equal(JSON.parse(child.stdinPayload).options.compiledRouteTopOnly, true);`. Run `VP` and see the new test fail because the value is `undefined`. Then in `PJ`'s `advisorCliRequest`, inside the `requestOptions` object, directly after the line `includeAbstainReasons: true,`, add the line `compiledRouteTopOnly: true,` and above it a comment saying the brief prints the head and at most one partner, and every hook asks for the route of the top pick alone, so a partner that carried a command here and none there would give two runtimes two heads for one request. Verify: the first run fails as described, then `VP` reports `fail 0`, `node --check $PJ` exits 0 and `rg -c -F 'compiledRouteTopOnly: true,' $PJ` prints 1. When another `PJT` test fails because it pins the request options, update that one expectation to hold the new key and name the test in the task note. Evidence: worker log: first run `ℹ fail 1` on the new case (`+ undefined`, `- true`), then `ℹ pass 45`, `ℹ fail 0`. Opus check PASS 2026-11-14: `VP` rerun 45 of 45 three times, `node --check` exit 0, `rg -c -F 'compiledRouteTopOnly: true,'` printed 1. The timing case `termination grace stays inside the configured timeout budget` failed once in the worker run and passed every rerun, a timing flake outside this task.\n77-\n78-### Lane D: the two documents that show the line\n79-- [x] T007 [P] Show the companion line with its policy word in the skill README (`RM`). After T004. Replace ``the brief adds `Also: /doctor:<command> <target>` as its second line`` with ``the brief adds `Also: /doctor:<command> <target> (<policy>)` as its second line, where the policy is run, start or ask, and leaves the line out when the head already names that command``. Verify: `rg -c -F 'Also: /doctor:<command> <target> (<policy>)' $RM` prints 1. Evidence: coordinator check 2026-11-14: `G diff -U0 -- $RM` shows the one sentence replaced word for word and nothing else, and the verify `rg -c` printed 1. Noted, not changed: a child session prints `parent-named only` in place of the policy word, which the README sentence does not say.\n80-- [x] T008 [P] Show the companion line with its policy word in the hook reference (`HK`). After T004. In the paragraph that starts `The injected brief begins with`, find the sentence that says a doctor command appears on the top line or under a skill, and add after it: ``The line under a skill reads `Also: <command> (<policy>)`, and it is left out when the head or its partner already names that command.`` Verify: `rg -c -F 'Also: <command> (<policy>)' $HK` prints 1 and the added sentence holds no em dash and no semicolon. Evidence: coordinator check 2026-11-14: `G diff -U0 -- $HK` shows the one sentence added after the doctor-command sentence and nothing else, `rg -c -F 'Also: <command> (<policy>)'` printed 1, and the sentence holds no em dash and no semicolon.\n--\n87:<!-- FILL: Write one verification task per requirement. Each one runs a command. GOOD: T003 Run both invocations. Verify: `ls logs/archive | wc -l` prints 3 and 10. WEAK: T003 Test everything. -->\n88-\n89-- [x] T009 [C] Rebuild and run every suite (`$SP/after.txt`). Run `npm run build` in `ADV`, `TC`, `VALL`, `AI_SESSION_CHILD=1 npx vitest run tests/hooks/skill-advisor-cli-fallback-envelope.vitest.ts tests/render-child-session.vitest.ts` in `ADV` and `VP`, and write each command with its result lines into `after.txt`. Verify: the build and `TC` exit 0, `VALL` reports 0 failed with more passed tests than `$SP/before.txt`, the child-flag run reports 0 failed and `VP` reports `fail 0`. Then run `REAL` and write its lines beside their `before.txt` lines, or `embedder not live`. A lower real count does not block this task: when the `combined` count of the real sealed plain line is lower, write `DROP: real plain holdout <before> to <after>` and report the line to the coordinator's goal log row in T013. Evidence: `after.txt`: build exit 0, `TC` exit 0, `VALL` `Tests  1922 passed | 6 skipped (1928)` 0 failed against 1919, the child-flag run `Tests  16 passed (16)`, `VP` `ℹ fail 0` of 45. `REAL` lines equal `before.txt`, plain holdout 4 of 44, no `DROP`.\n90-- [x] T010 [C] Hold every floor (`$SP/after.txt`). Run `FLOOR` and `EVAL` and write each beside its `before.txt` line. The typed recall, false offer and typed delivery lines and the `EVAL` line must equal their before lines. The plain tune line and the sealed plain line may each differ only by a higher `attached` or `combined` count, which is written as `GAIN: plain tune <before> to <after>` or `GAIN: plain holdout <before> to <after>` and reported to the coordinator, who recaptures the committed baseline with its own script. A sealed plain `combined` count below its before line means the change lowered reach on requests nobody tuned for, and it blocks. Verify: three `FLOOR` lines and the `EVAL` line equal their `before.txt` lines character for character, the plain tune `combined` and the sealed plain `combined` are not lower, and `rg -c -e \"^RESULT: every floor holds$\" $SP/after.txt` prints 1. When a line is lower, mark this task `[B]`. Evidence: `after.txt`: the typed recall, false offer and typed delivery lines and `[161,51,21,5]` equal `before.txt`, plain tune 28 of 45 and sealed plain 1 of 44 equal and not lower, no `GAIN`. `rg -c -e '^RESULT: every floor holds$'` printed 1.\n91:- [x] T011 [C] Rerun the head probe (`$SP/repro-after.txt`). Run `HEADS` after the build. Verify: `rg -c -F 'same head: true' $SP/repro-after.txt` prints 17, `rg -c -F 'same head: false' $SP/repro-after.txt` prints nothing, `rg -c -F 'use sk-doc → /create:manual-testing-playbook (start) 0.95/0.12 pass.' $SP/repro-after.txt` prints 2, `rg -c -F 'command-doctor-' $SP/repro-after.txt` prints nothing, `rg -c -e 'Also: /[a-z:-]+( [a-z-]+)?\\s*$' $SP/repro-after.txt` prints nothing, `rg -c -F 'Also: /doctor:update' $SP/repro-after.txt` prints nothing, `rg -c -F 'vs sk-doc 0.93/0.12 pass.' $SP/repro-after.txt` prints 2, `rg -c -F 'vs sk-doc → /create:readme (start)' $SP/repro-after.txt` prints nothing and `rg -c -F 'ambiguous: sk-doc → /create:manual-testing-playbook (start) 0.95/0.16 vs system-deep-loop 0.92/0.12 pass.' $SP/repro-after.txt` prints 2. The last three checks read the two ambiguous prompts: after T014 the partner row is the same on both paths. Evidence: `repro-after.txt`: `same head: true` 17, `same head: false` nothing, the playbook head 2, `command-doctor-` nothing, bare `Also:` nothing, `Also: /doctor:update` nothing, `vs sk-doc 0.93/0.12 pass.` 2, `vs sk-doc → /create:readme (start)` nothing, the ambiguous playbook line 2.\n92-- [x] T012 [C] Check the boundaries (`$SP/boundaries.txt`). Write each command below with its output or exit code into `boundaries.txt`. Verify: `G diff --quiet BASE -- AGENTS.md` exits 0, `G diff --stat BASE -- '*package.json' '*package-lock.json'` prints nothing, `G diff BASE -U0 -- .skilled .opencode | rg \"^\\+.*(//|#|\\*).*(specs/|REQ-0|ADR-0|T0[0-9]{2}|phase [0-9]|A-[0-9]|B-[0-9])\"` prints nothing and `G diff --name-only BASE -- .skilled .opencode` names only `HR`, `RN`, `PJ`, `HRT`, `DBR`, `PJT`, `RM` and `HK`, plus one golden test file when T004 named it. Evidence: `boundaries.txt`: `AGENTS.md` diff exit 0, the package diff printed nothing, the comment id search exited 1, `--name-only` names exactly `HR`, `RN`, `PJ`, `HRT`, `DBR`, `PJT`, `RM` and `HK`, no golden file.\n93-- [x] T013 [C] Write the results and close the phase (`$PH/implementation-summary.md`). Copy `$SP/implementation-summary.scaffold.md` to `$PH/implementation-summary.md` and fill it: `BASE`, the files changed, the before and after head lines for the two prompts that differed, the floor lines and any `GAIN` or `DROP` line. Mark each row of `acceptance-criteria.md` with the evidence observed, set the status in `spec.md` and the phase 21 row of `../spec.md` to `Complete`, append one row to the parent goal log with `node .skilled/hooks/goal/bin/goal.cjs packet-log specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build \"<item> | <state> | <evidence>\" --workspace \"$PWD\"`, then run `node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/repair-derived.cjs --folder $PH --apply`. Verify: `bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/validate.sh $PH --strict` prints `RESULT: PASSED` and `rg -c \"\\| Unmet \\|\" $PH/acceptance-criteria.md` prints nothing. Evidence: `implementation-summary.md` filled with `BASE` `7281629941`, the eight files, the before and after heads of the three prompts that differed, the floor lines and no `GAIN` or `DROP`. Nine acceptance rows Met, `rg -c '| Unmet |'` prints nothing. Status Complete in `spec.md` and the phase 21 row of `../spec.md`. Goal log row `Phase 021 brief head parity closed` written, cut-row count unchanged. `repair-derived.cjs --apply` run, then `validate.sh --strict` printed `RESULT: PASSED`.\n94-<!-- /ANCHOR:phase-3 -->\n95-\n--\n102:- [x] All tasks marked `[x]` Evidence: every task line T001 to T014 is `[x]` after T013.\n103-- [x] No `[B]` blocked tasks remaining Evidence: `rg -c '^- \\[B\\]'` over this file prints nothing.\n104-- [x] `scratch/after.txt` ends with `RESULT: every floor holds` Evidence: `rg -c '^RESULT: every floor holds$' scratch/after.txt` printed 1, its last line.\n105-- [x] `scratch/repro-after.txt` prints `same head: true` 17 times Evidence: `rg -c -F 'same head: true' scratch/repro-after.txt` printed 17.\n106-<!-- /ANCHOR:completion -->\n--\n169:- [x] CHK-022 [P0] Each of T003 to T006 and T014 recorded a failing first run for the reason its line names. Evidence: worker logs, confirmed by each Opus check: T003 `1 failed | 6 passed`, T004 `3 failed | 6 passed`, T005 `2 failed | 9 passed`, T006 `ℹ fail 1`, T014 `ℹ fail 1`, each on the reason its line names.\n170-- [x] CHK-023 [P1] The child-flag run reports 0 failed, so the companion keeps its child marker (T009). Evidence: the child-flag run printed `Tests  16 passed (16)` (T009).\n171-<!-- /ANCHOR:testing -->\n172-\n173----\n--\n179:- [x] CHK-FIX-001 [P0] Finding classes: A-2 and A-3 are `cross-consumer` (one handler, two request shapes), the OpenCode label is `instance-only`, A-7 and the second half of B-9 are `class-of-bug` in the companion line. Evidence: the plan addendum classes the findings as written here, and T003, T005, T006 and T014 fix them in place.\n180-- [x] CHK-FIX-002 [P0] Same-class producers: `rg -n \"recommendations\\[0\\]\" .opencode/plugins/system-skill-advisor.js .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks --glob '!**/tests/**'` is read, and every hit that labels a head takes a passing row. Evidence: the search lists the plugin line 615, which now takes the first passing row, and `hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.ts:195` in `skillLabelFor`, which reads `metadata.skillLabel` first and row 0 only when it is missing. That fallback is listed in Known Limitations and carried to the packet review.\n181-- [x] CHK-FIX-003 [P0] Consumers: `rg -n \"compiledRouteTopOnly\" .skilled .opencode --glob '!**/tests/**' --glob '!**/dist/**'` lists every caller of the option, and each still gets the head it got or a head with the command restored. Evidence: the search lists the plugin line 858, the Claude hook fallback line 241, the handler lines 632 and 707, the schema, CLI and manifest. The handler test passes 7 of 7 and the probe prints the same head on both paths, the command restored on the playbook prompt.\n182:- [x] CHK-FIX-004 [P1] Matrix: request shape (top only, every row) by head kind (hub with a route, command row, doctor bridge) by companion (none, distinct, repeat), covered by T003 to T005 and the probe. Evidence: top-only and every-row by hub head are covered by `HRT` (7 passed), command-row and doctor-bridge heads with no, distinct and repeated companion by `DBR` (11 passed), and the probe prints 17 same heads.\n183-- [x] CHK-FIX-005 [P1] Hostile state: the child-flag run in T009 proves the companion under `AI_SESSION_CHILD=1`. Evidence: the child-flag run under `AI_SESSION_CHILD=1` printed 16 of 16 (T009).\n184-- [x] CHK-FIX-006 [P1] Evidence names `BASE`: `$PH/implementation-summary.md` names the commit T001 recorded. Evidence: `implementation-summary.md` names `7281629941` as `BASE`.\n185-<!-- /ANCHOR:fix-completeness -->\n186-\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"48:Worker lanes. Lane H owns `HR` and `HRT` and runs T003. Lane R owns `RN` and `DBR` and runs T004 then T005. Lane P owns `PJ` and `PJT` and runs T006 then T014. Lane D owns `RM` and `HK` and starts after T004. The lanes own different files and run in parallel after T002. `[P]` marks a task that may start beside another lane's task. T005 and T014 carry no `[P]`, because each waits for the task before it in its lane.\n49-<!-- /ANCHOR:notation -->\n50-\n51----\n52-\n--\n68:- [x] T003 [P] Keep the command on the head row under every request shape (`HR`, `HRT`). Test first: in `HRT`, in the case `lets the top hub carry a command only a lower row would offer`, replace the last assertion `expect(everyRow.find((row) => row.skillId === 'sk-doc')?.command).toBeUndefined();` with `expect(everyRow[0]?.skillId).toBe('sk-doc');`, `expect(everyRow[0]?.command).toBe(command);` and `expect(everyRow[0]?.command).toBe(topOnly[0]?.command);`, and rename the case to `lets the top hub carry its command under every request shape`. Leave the case `never offers one command through both its own row and the owning hub` as it is, because it pins a hub that is not the head. Run `VX tests/handlers/advisor-recommend-compiled-route-option.vitest.ts` and see the renamed case fail with `everyRow[0].command` undefined. Then in `HR`'s `enrichCompiledRoutes`, replace `(topOnly && index === 0)` with `index === 0`, and replace the three comment lines that start `// A caller that asks for the top pick alone renders that head` with a comment saying every caller renders the head and at most one partner, so a command only a lower row carries would never reach the reader, and the head keeps it whichever shape the caller asked for. Verify: the first run fails as described, then `VX tests/handlers/advisor-recommend-compiled-route-option.vitest.ts` reports 0 failed, `rg -c \"topOnly && index === 0\" $HR` prints nothing and `TC` exits 0. When the untouched case fails because its hub now leads, mark the task `[B]` with the row order printed. Evidence: worker log: first run `Tests  1 failed | 6 passed (7)`, after the change `Tests  7 passed (7)`. Opus check PASS 2026-11-14: `VX` rerun `Tests  7 passed (7)`, `rg -c \"topOnly && index === 0\"` printed nothing, `TC` exit 0, the hub that is not the head is still suppressed.\n69-\n70-### Lane R: the renderer\n71-- [x] T004 [P] Print the policy word on the companion line (`RN`, `DBR`). Test first: in `DBR`, import `commandRowFor` from `../lib/command-attach.js` when absent. In the case `keeps the complete companion before directives under the default cap`, change the expected head to `` `Advisor: live; use sk-git 0.82/0.20 pass.\\nAlso: /doctor:git setup (${commandRowFor(<the bridge id that case passes>)?.policy})` ``, and in the case `uses the first doctor bridge after the top pick, past other skills`, change `'\\nAlso: /doctor:update'` to `'\\nAlso: /doctor:update (ask)'`. Add `it('prints the policy word of a companion and the child marker in a child session')`, which renders `advisorResult([{ skill: 'system-spec-kit', kind: 'skill', confidence: 0.95, uncertainty: 0.12 }, { skill: 'command-doctor-env', kind: 'command', confidence: 0.82, uncertainty: 0.2 }])` twice, with `{ childSession: false }` and with `{ childSession: true }`, expecting `\\nAlso: /doctor:env list (run)` both times because a read-only run stays visible to a child, and renders the same head with `command-doctor-update` under `{ childSession: true }` expecting `\\nAlso: /doctor:update (parent-named only)`. Run `VX tests/doctor-brief-render.vitest.ts` and see the three expectations fail on the missing policy word. Then in `RN`'s `companionCommandLabel`, build the label as the command followed by ` (<word>)`, where the word is `policyWord(row?.policy, row?.mutating, childSession)`, and return the command alone when that word is `null`. Replace the comment above the function with one saying a companion is a command the reader may act on, so it carries the same policy word the head carries, and a child still gets the downgrade marker. Verify: the first run fails as described, then `VX tests/doctor-brief-render.vitest.ts`, `VX tests/render-child-session.vitest.ts`, `VX tests/command-head-length.vitest.ts` and `VX tests/command-brief-golden.vitest.ts` report 0 failed and `TC` exits 0. When a golden brief in another file pins a companion with no policy word, update that one expectation and name the file in the task note. Evidence: worker log: first run `Tests  3 failed | 6 passed (9)` on the missing policy word, then `Tests  9 passed (9)`. Opus check PASS 2026-11-14: `VX` reruns `doctor-brief-render` 9 of 9, `render-child-session` 12 of 12, `command-head-length` 120 of 120, `command-brief-golden` 13 of 13, `TC` exit 0, no other golden file holds `Also: /`.\n72-- [x] T005 Drop a companion that repeats a printed command (`RN`, `DBR`). After T004. Test first: in `DBR`, add `it('prints a command once when the head already names it')`, which renders a head row `{ skill: 'system-skilled-doctor', kind: 'skill', confidence: 0.82, uncertainty: 0.26, command: '/doctor:update', policy: 'ask', ownerSkillId: 'system-skilled-doctor' }` followed by `{ skill: 'command-doctor-update', kind: 'command', confidence: 0.82, uncertainty: 0.2 }`, widening `RecommendationInput` with the three optional fields, and expects the brief to contain `/doctor:update` exactly once and no `Also:`. Add `it('prints a second doctor bridge when the first repeats the head')`, with a third row `command-doctor-env`, expecting `\\nAlso: /doctor:env list (run)`. Run `VX tests/doctor-brief-render.vitest.ts` and see the first case print `Also: /doctor:update (ask)`. Then in `RN`'s `renderAdvisorBrief`, compute the head's printed command as `top.command ?? (top.needsMode === true ? undefined : commandRowFor(top.skill)?.command)`, and make the companion pick skip a row whose `doctorCommandString(row.skill)` equals it. In the ambiguous branch, also print no companion whose command equals the partner's `vs.command ?? commandRowFor(vs.skill)?.command`. Add a comment saying a command printed twice reads as two offers. Verify: the first run fails as described, then `VX tests/doctor-brief-render.vitest.ts` and `VX tests/command-brief-golden.vitest.ts` report 0 failed and `TC` exits 0. Evidence: worker log: first run `Tests  2 failed | 9 passed (11)`, one on `[ '/doctor:update', '/doctor:update' ]` printed twice, then 24 of 24 across the two files. Opus check PASS 2026-11-14: `VX tests/doctor-brief-render.vitest.ts` 11 of 11, `tests/command-brief-golden.vitest.ts` 13 of 13, `TC` exit 0, a render probe printed no repeated companion and kept a distinct one.\n--\n75:- [x] T006 [P] Label the OpenCode head from the first passing row (`PJ`, `PJT`). Test first: in `PJT`, add `test('names the first passing row when the first row does not pass')`, which saves and deletes `process.env.AI_SESSION_CHILD`, builds the plugin with `makePlugin({ spawnOverride: spawnSequence([fakeChild({ stdout: cliEnvelope({ recommendations: [{ skillId: 'command-doctor-sk-skill-convert', score: 0.5, confidence: 0.8, uncertainty: 0.42, command: '/doctor:sk-skill convert', policy: 'ask', ownerSkillId: 'sk-doc' }, { skillId: 'sk-doc', score: 0.8, confidence: 0.95, uncertainty: 0.12 }] }) })]) })`, awaits `runPrompt(hooks)`, restores the variable in a `finally` and asserts that `output.system[0]` contains `use sk-doc 0.95/0.12 pass.` and does not contain `command-doctor-`. Run `VP` and see it fail with `use command-doctor-sk-skill-convert 0.95/0.12 pass.` Then in `PJ`, in the function that parses the CLI payload, replace `const top = recommendations[0] ?? null;` with `const top = recommendations.find((recommendation) => recommendation.passes_threshold) ?? recommendations[0] ?? null;` and add a comment saying the renderer leads with the first passing row, so a label taken from a row that does not pass would name one row and score another. Verify: the first run fails as described, then `VP` reports `fail 0`, `node --check $PJ` exits 0 and `rg -c \"const top = recommendations\\[0\\] \\?\\? null;\" $PJ` prints nothing. Evidence: worker log: first run `ℹ fail 1` with `Advisor: live; use command-doctor-sk-skill-convert 0.95/0.12 pass.`, then `ℹ pass 44`, `ℹ fail 0`. Opus check PASS 2026-11-14: `VP` rerun 44 of 44, `node --check` exit 0, the old `const top` line gone. Accepted deviation: the case passes `freshness: 'live'`, as every other brief case in `PJT` does, because without it the plugin renders the outage line and the case fails for the wrong reason.\n76:- [x] T014 Send the top-only route option from the OpenCode plugin (`PJ`, `PJT`). After T006. This task changes the function `advisorCliRequest` and nothing else. Test first: in `PJT`, add `test('asks the advisor for the route of the top pick alone, as the hooks do')`, which builds `const child = fakeChild({ stdout: cliEnvelope({ freshness: 'live', recommendations: [] }) });` and `const hooks = await makePlugin({ spawnOverride: spawnSequence([child]) });`, awaits `runPrompt(hooks)` and asserts `assert.equal(JSON.parse(child.stdinPayload).options.compiledRouteTopOnly, true);`. Run `VP` and see the new test fail because the value is `undefined`. Then in `PJ`'s `advisorCliRequest`, inside the `requestOptions` object, directly after the line `includeAbstainReasons: true,`, add the line `compiledRouteTopOnly: true,` and above it a comment saying the brief prints the head and at most one partner, and every hook asks for the route of the top pick alone, so a partner that carried a command here and none there would give two runtimes two heads for one request. Verify: the first run fails as described, then `VP` reports `fail 0`, `node --check $PJ` exits 0 and `rg -c -F 'compiledRouteTopOnly: true,' $PJ` prints 1. When another `PJT` test fails because it pins the request options, update that one expectation to hold the new key and name the test in the task note. Evidence: worker log: first run `ℹ fail 1` on the new case (`+ undefined`, `- true`), then `ℹ pass 45`, `ℹ fail 0`. Opus check PASS 2026-11-14: `VP` rerun 45 of 45 three times, `node --check` exit 0, `rg -c -F 'compiledRouteTopOnly: true,'` printed 1. The timing case `termination grace stays inside the configured timeout budget` failed once in the worker run and passed every rerun, a timing flake outside this task.\n77-\n78-### Lane D: the two documents that show the line\n79-- [x] T007 [P] Show the companion line with its policy word in the skill README (`RM`). After T004. Replace ``the brief adds `Also: /doctor:<command> <target>` as its second line`` with ``the brief adds `Also: /doctor:<command> <target> (<policy>)` as its second line, where the policy is run, start or ask, and leaves the line out when the head already names that command``. Verify: `rg -c -F 'Also: /doctor:<command> <target> (<policy>)' $RM` prints 1. Evidence: coordinator check 2026-11-14: `G diff -U0 -- $RM` shows the one sentence replaced word for word and nothing else, and the verify `rg -c` printed 1. Noted, not changed: a child session prints `parent-named only` in place of the policy word, which the README sentence does not say.\n80-- [x] T008 [P] Show the companion line with its policy word in the hook reference (`HK`). After T004. In the paragraph that starts `The injected brief begins with`, find the sentence that says a doctor command appears on the top line or under a skill, and add after it: ``The line under a skill reads `Also: <command> (<policy>)`, and it is left out when the head or its partner already names that command.`` Verify: `rg -c -F 'Also: <command> (<policy>)' $HK` prints 1 and the added sentence holds no em dash and no semicolon. Evidence: coordinator check 2026-11-14: `G diff -U0 -- $HK` shows the one sentence added after the doctor-command sentence and nothing else, `rg -c -F 'Also: <command> (<policy>)'` printed 1, and the sentence holds no em dash and no semicolon.\n--\n87:<!-- FILL: Write one verification task per requirement. Each one runs a command. GOOD: T003 Run both invocations. Verify: `ls logs/archive | wc -l` prints 3 and 10. WEAK: T003 Test everything. -->\n88-\n89-- [x] T009 [C] Rebuild and run every suite (`$SP/after.txt`). Run `npm run build` in `ADV`, `TC`, `VALL`, `AI_SESSION_CHILD=1 npx vitest run tests/hooks/skill-advisor-cli-fallback-envelope.vitest.ts tests/render-child-session.vitest.ts` in `ADV` and `VP`, and write each command with its result lines into `after.txt`. Verify: the build and `TC` exit 0, `VALL` reports 0 failed with more passed tests than `$SP/before.txt`, the child-flag run reports 0 failed and `VP` reports `fail 0`. Then run `REAL` and write its lines beside their `before.txt` lines, or `embedder not live`. A lower real count does not block this task: when the `combined` count of the real sealed plain line is lower, write `DROP: real plain holdout <before> to <after>` and report the line to the coordinator's goal log row in T013. Evidence: `after.txt`: build exit 0, `TC` exit 0, `VALL` `Tests  1922 passed | 6 skipped (1928)` 0 failed against 1919, the child-flag run `Tests  16 passed (16)`, `VP` `ℹ fail 0` of 45. `REAL` lines equal `before.txt`, plain holdout 4 of 44, no `DROP`.\n90-- [x] T010 [C] Hold every floor (`$SP/after.txt`). Run `FLOOR` and `EVAL` and write each beside its `before.txt` line. The typed recall, false offer and typed delivery lines and the `EVAL` line must equal their before lines. The plain tune line and the sealed plain line may each differ only by a higher `attached` or `combined` count, which is written as `GAIN: plain tune <before> to <after>` or `GAIN: plain holdout <before> to <after>` and reported to the coordinator, who recaptures the committed baseline with its own script. A sealed plain `combined` count below its before line means the change lowered reach on requests nobody tuned for, and it blocks. Verify: three `FLOOR` lines and the `EVAL` line equal their `before.txt` lines character for character, the plain tune `combined` and the sealed plain `combined` are not lower, and `rg -c -e \"^RESULT: every floor holds$\" $SP/after.txt` prints 1. When a line is lower, mark this task `[B]`. Evidence: `after.txt`: the typed recall, false offer and typed delivery lines and `[161,51,21,5]` equal `before.txt`, plain tune 28 of 45 and sealed plain 1 of 44 equal and not lower, no `GAIN`. `rg -c -e '^RESULT: every floor holds$'` printed 1.\n91:- [x] T011 [C] Rerun the head probe (`$SP/repro-after.txt`). Run `HEADS` after the build. Verify: `rg -c -F 'same head: true' $SP/repro-after.txt` prints 17, `rg -c -F 'same head: false' $SP/repro-after.txt` prints nothing, `rg -c -F 'use sk-doc → /create:manual-testing-playbook (start) 0.95/0.12 pass.' $SP/repro-after.txt` prints 2, `rg -c -F 'command-doctor-' $SP/repro-after.txt` prints nothing, `rg -c -e 'Also: /[a-z:-]+( [a-z-]+)?\\s*$' $SP/repro-after.txt` prints nothing, `rg -c -F 'Also: /doctor:update' $SP/repro-after.txt` prints nothing, `rg -c -F 'vs sk-doc 0.93/0.12 pass.' $SP/repro-after.txt` prints 2, `rg -c -F 'vs sk-doc → /create:readme (start)' $SP/repro-after.txt` prints nothing and `rg -c -F 'ambiguous: sk-doc → /create:manual-testing-playbook (start) 0.95/0.16 vs system-deep-loop 0.92/0.12 pass.' $SP/repro-after.txt` prints 2. The last three checks read the two ambiguous prompts: after T014 the partner row is the same on both paths. Evidence: `repro-after.txt`: `same head: true` 17, `same head: false` nothing, the playbook head 2, `command-doctor-` nothing, bare `Also:` nothing, `Also: /doctor:update` nothing, `vs sk-doc 0.93/0.12 pass.` 2, `vs sk-doc → /create:readme (start)` nothing, the ambiguous playbook line 2.\n92-- [x] T012 [C] Check the boundaries (`$SP/boundaries.txt`). Write each command below with its output or exit code into `boundaries.txt`. Verify: `G diff --quiet BASE -- AGENTS.md` exits 0, `G diff --stat BASE -- '*package.json' '*package-lock.json'` prints nothing, `G diff BASE -U0 --